{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WnrtiTg7TMLLgmjWHKiFQb","type":"tool_result","content":"80\twhere $\\beta$ controls the ISR bonus weight. We study $\\beta \\in \\{0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3\\}$.\n81\t\n82\t\\subsection{Hacking Mode Taxonomy}\n83\t\n84\tThrough manual inspection and automated classification of degenerate proofs, we identify 9 reward hacking modes across FOL and Prolog:\n85\t\n86\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n87\t\\item \\textbf{H1} (Conclusion Embedding): The conclusion is embedded verbatim in the premise list, making the proof trivially derivable. ISR$\\approx$0.\n88\t\\item \\textbf{H7a} (Explicit Tautology): Proof reduces to $P \\therefore P$. Single premise, ISR$\\approx$0.5.\n89\t\\item \\textbf{H7b} (Obfuscated Tautology): Semantically tautological but syntactically disguised to evade pattern detectors. Meta-Goodhart: gaming the diagnostic itself.\n90\t\\item \\textbf{H8} (Conjunction Collapse): Proof retains original structure but adds redundant conjunctive components, inflating length while reducing per-component necessity.\n91\t\\item \\textbf{H9} (Contradiction Injection): Premises contain contradictions, enabling the solver to derive anything (ex falso quodlibet).\n92\t\\item \\textbf{PG} (Proof Grafting): Components from unrelated proved lemmas are grafted into the proof, passing execution but contributing no logical value.\n93\t\\item \\textbf{DS} (Degenerate Syntax): Prolog-specific. Syntactically malformed but accepted by the solver's parser.\n94\t\\item \\textbf{QF} (Query-as-Fact): Prolog-specific. The query is asserted as a fact in the knowledge base.\n95\t\\item \\textbf{H7a$_{\\text{Prolog}}$}: Prolog variant of explicit tautology, exploiting Prolog's backtracking semantics.\n96\t\\end{itemize}\n97\t\n98\t%% ============================================================\n99\t\\section{Experiments}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ecf0ac2-b3f3-4af8-8613-d844ba4d1825","uuid":"b69c6f81-0741-422d-a592-7e26dff824ff","timestamp":"2026-05-21T22:31:27.464Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/solver-trace-ablation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T214001/best/paper.tex","content":"where $\\beta$ controls the ISR bonus weight. We study $\\beta \\in \\{0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3\\}$.\n\n\\subsection{Hacking Mode Taxonomy}\n\nThrough manual inspection and automated classification of degenerate proofs, we identify 9 reward hacking modes across FOL and Prolog:\n\n\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n\\item \\textbf{H1} (Conclusion Embedding): The conclusion is embedded verbatim in the premise list, making the proof trivially derivable. ISR$\\approx$0.\n\\item \\textbf{H7a} (Explicit Tautology): Proof reduces to $P \\therefore P$. Single premise, ISR$\\approx$0.5.\n\\item \\textbf{H7b} (Obfuscated Tautology): Semantically tautological but syntactically disguised to evade pattern detectors. Meta-Goodhart: gaming the diagnostic itself.\n\\item \\textbf{H8} (Conjunction Collapse): Proof retains original structure but adds redundant conjunctive components, inflating length while reducing per-component necessity.\n\\item \\textbf{H9} (Contradiction Injection): Premises contain contradictions, enabling the solver to derive anything (ex falso quodlibet).\n\\item \\textbf{PG} (Proof Grafting): Components from unrelated proved lemmas are grafted into the proof, passing execution but contributing no logical value.\n\\item \\textbf{DS} (Degenerate Syntax): Prolog-specific. Syntactically malformed but accepted by the solver's parser.\n\\item \\textbf{QF} (Query-as-Fact): Prolog-specific. The query is asserted as a fact in the knowledge base.\n\\item \\textbf{H7a$_{\\text{Prolog}}$}: Prolog variant of explicit tautology, exploiting Prolog's backtracking semantics.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n%% ============================================================\n\\section{Experiments}","numLines":20,"startLine":80,"totalLines":381}}}
